WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.325
Interactions are useful when they make learners think, decide, compare, practice,

00:00:04.325 --> 00:00:05.000
or apply.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Click-to-reveal elements, tabs, markers, accordions, process interactions,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.999
and branching scenarios can break content into manageable pieces.

00:00:11.999 --> 00:00:14.950
They should not be added only because a screen looks too quiet.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.000
Extra clicks without purpose can create friction instead of engagement.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
A simple interaction can work well when the objective is exploration or organization.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
For example, tabs may separate roles, phases, risks, or examples.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.950
Markers can call attention to parts of an interface or diagram.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.985
A process interaction can show sequence.

00:00:30.985 --> 00:00:34.000
These patterns are effective when the learner needs to understand relationships,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.985
not when the designer simply wants to hide text.

00:00:35.985 --> 00:00:38.950
Scenarios are powerful because they place learning in context.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.000
A decision-based scenario asks learners to choose an action, see feedback,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:43.945
and understand consequences.

00:00:43.945 --> 00:00:46.950
The scenario does not need to be dramatic to be useful.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.590
It should reflect realistic decisions, common mistakes,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.245
and the judgment learners need on the job.

00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:53.965
Feedback should explain why an answer is strong or weak,

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:56.140
not just say correct or incorrect.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.000
Software demonstrations and simulations can help when learners need procedural practice.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.950
Keep them focused on the task, not every feature in the application.

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:04.985
Align every interaction with an objective.

00:01:04.985 --> 00:01:07.950
If an interaction does not support the outcome, simplify it.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.950
The best practice activity feels like a rehearsal for real work rather than an obstacle

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.950
placed between the learner and the next screen.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.950
When designing practice, begin with the mistake learners are likely to make.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.950
Then create an interaction that helps them recognize and correct that mistake.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:24.000
This keeps the activity grounded in real performance instead of novelty.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:24.445
A small,

00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:28.300
well-targeted decision can teach more than a complex interaction that does not connect to

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:28.870
the job.
